# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #2J - A measured guard for the reflection route
# ===========================================================================
#
# Paying a deferred debt: guarding a route with a loss the code measures about itself, rather than a law fitted to its behaviour.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# The previous stage found a routine returning 3.09e4 relative error and
# DELIBERATELY DID NOT GUARD IT, on the grounds that calibrating a threshold
# needed a sweep there was no budget for, and that a guessed threshold
# 'would have looked like progress and been worse than the note'.
#
# This stage paid that debt. A deferral is only honest if it is actually a
# deferral.
#
# MEASURED, NOT MODELLED. The obvious guard is the documented law
# 1e-16 * exp(|z|), which the module's own notes say runs one to two decades
# pessimistic. Instead the ascending series now reports its OWN
# cancellation:
#
#     loss = max|term| / |sum|
#
# the largest quantity the series forms, over the answer it produces. The
# reflection then multiplies that by its own combination loss. There is no
# fitted constant inside the indicator; the only tuned number is the
# threshold it is compared against, and that is stated with its margin.
#
# THE FIRST CALIBRATION FAILED, AND THE INSTRUMENT WAS AT FAULT AGAIN. A
# 2480-point sweep of actual-error-versus-indicator gave a worst ratio of
# 3.1e14, which read as 'the indicator is worthless'. It was the METRIC:
# relative error is unbounded near the ZEROS of Y, so that sweep was
# measuring the reference rather than the routine.
#
# That was the FOURTH distinct time in this project that an instrument,
# rather than a routine, was the thing at fault. It is the single most
# common way to be wrong here.
#
# CALIBRATED AT CHARACTERISED POINTS - places where the true error was
# already known from the previous stage:
#
#     nu = 36.8, z = 54.46   err 3.09e4    loss*eps 1.02      refuse
#     nu = 36.8, z = 47.84   err 2.18      loss*eps 9.1e-3    refuse
#     nu = 20.5, z = 30.34   err 7.8e-8    loss*eps 1.9e-6    allow
#     nu = 12.3, z = 16.60   err 7.5e-12   loss*eps 1.2e-11   allow
#
# The threshold 1e-3 separates every measured case with the closest allowed
# one 500x inside it. THE INDICATOR IS NOT A PROVEN BOUND - at
# nu = 36.8, z = 47.84 the actual error is 240x larger than loss*eps - and
# the threshold carries that margin explicitly rather than pretending
# otherwise.
#
# THE TEST MOVED, NOT THE THRESHOLD. An existing check used nu = 1/2,
# z = 30, which the new guard refuses. The tempting repair is to loosen the
# threshold until the suite is green - and it would have been wrong, because
# the module ALREADY DOCUMENTED Y as 'unusable past 30'. Loosening a guard
# to preserve a test is how a guard becomes decoration.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is posim notebook source. Lines beginning with '#' are
# comments and are ignored by the interpreter; every other line is a command
# that will be executed in order.
#
# From the repository root (the directory holding Cargo.toml), run ONE of:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2J.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --script   StageNbks/Stage_2J.posim
#
#   --notebook  prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript, like a Mathematica
#               notebook. This is the mode to use when reading along.
#   --script    prints the same transcript; the two differ only in how the
#               prompt lines are laid out. Either is reproducible.
#
# To work through it by hand instead, start the interactive interpreter:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release
#
# and paste the command lines one at a time. Type HELP for the full command
# language, and %quit to leave.
#
# First run will compile the workspace (a few minutes). Later runs are
# immediate. If 'cargo' is not found, install Rust from https://rustup.rs .
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BROWSER GUI, AND WHY IT IS NOT USED BY THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# posim has a live 3-D scene window served over HTTP and viewed in a browser.
# THIS NOTEBOOK DOES NOT OPEN IT, and you should not try to, because it would
# be empty. The reason is exact:
#
#   The window renders SIMULATOR BODIES - points, spheres, cuboids, tori,
#   disks, cylinders, dumbbells - by sending their positions each frame.
#   This notebook creates none. Running 'scene create' here prints
#
#       showing 0 entities
#
#   and 'scene start' then advances a world containing nothing: the Start,
#   Pause, Stop and Reverse buttons all respond, and nothing moves, because
#   there is nothing to move.
#
# That is not a defect to be worked around. The subject of this notebook has
# no position and no time coordinate, so there is nothing for a renderer to
# draw. Its results are the numbers and messages printed by the commands
# below, and those ARE the output.
#
# IF YOU WANT A WORKING SCENE WINDOW, use a notebook that builds bodies -
# StageNbks/Stage_2C.posim (six bodies in a box) and
# StageNbks/Stage_2H.posim (Newton's cradle and a settling ball) both open
# one automatically and start it running. The complete window procedure is
# written out inside each of those files.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANIMATION: NOT POSSIBLE HERE, AND THE EXACT REASON WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This stage cannot be animated, and the cause is precise rather than a
# matter of effort:
#
#   The scene window renders SIMULATOR ENTITIES. Concretely, it draws the
#   bodies held in the PhysicalObjectSystem - points, spheres, cuboids,
#   tori, disks, cylinders and dumbbells - by reading their positions and
#   orientations each frame and sending them over a websocket.
#
#   This stage evaluates SPECIAL FUNCTIONS. A call such as
#   bessel_j_nu(0.5, 2.0) creates no body, has no position, and has no time
#   coordinate: it is a pure function of its arguments. There is therefore
#   nothing for the renderer to draw, and 'scene create' on this session
#   would open a window showing an empty grid and the world axes.
#
#   That is not a defect and there is no workaround inside the scene window.
#   A function of a complex variable is not a moving object.
#
# What you CAN watch instead, and how, is written in the section above: the
# numbers printed by the command lines below change as you vary the order
# and the argument, and that variation IS the phenomenon this stage is about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# READ THIS FIRST - IT DETERMINES WHAT YOU WILL SEE.
#
# The guard added by this stage is on bessel_complex::bessel_y_nu, the
# single RAW route. The posim function 'bessel_y_nu(nu, z)' does NOT call
# it: the language is wired to bessel_cnu::bessel_y_cnu, the SELECTOR,
# which compares error estimates across several routes. So the lines below
# all return correct values rather than refusals - the selector never needed
# the guard.
#
# To see the guard REFUSE, you must call the raw route from Rust. From the
# repository root:
#
#     cargo test -p special_functions --release the_selector_is_accurate -- --nocapture
#
# That test asserts both halves: the selector is accurate at these points,
# AND the raw route refuses with a message naming the entry point to use
# instead. It is in special_functions/src/bessel_cnu.rs.
#
# Walk outward along the argument at fixed non-integer order. Through the
# language every one of these is answered correctly; the numbers are here so
# you can see the region the guard governs.

# Comfortably inside: the reflection has spent almost none of its precision.
bessel_y_nu(12.3, 16.6)

# Further out, still allowed: loss*eps is about 1.9e-6 here, and the answer
# is good to about 8e-8.
bessel_y_nu(20.5, 30.34)

# Past the threshold: this is where the RAW route now refuses, with a
# message stating how much precision it has spent and naming the entry
# point to use instead. Through the language you get the selector, so these
# print correct values (Cephes gives 6.801992e-2 for the second).
bessel_y_nu(36.8, 47.84)
bessel_y_nu(36.8, 54.464)

# The boundary case that moved an existing test. At nu = 1/2 the closed form
# is exact, yet at z = 30 the RAW reflection has spent 1.1e-3 of its
# precision - and the module's own accuracy notes already called Y
# 'unusable past 30'. Compare the two lines below against the elementary
# closed form that follows them.
bessel_y_nu(0.5, 22.0)
bessel_y_nu(0.5, 30.0)

# For comparison, the closed forms at nu = 1/2, which are elementary:
#     J_(1/2)(z) =  sqrt(2/(pi z)) sin z
#     Y_(1/2)(z) = -sqrt(2/(pi z)) cos z
bessel_j_nu(0.5, 22.0)
sqrt(2 / (3.14159265358979 * 22.0)) * sin(22.0)
-sqrt(2 / (3.14159265358979 * 22.0)) * cos(22.0)

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #2J NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
